    text Convert a single string or number to a complex number, or create a complex number from real and imaginary parts. @17,10462,663,40
    text Examples: @17,10514,663,20
    text >>> complex('+1.23') (1.23+0j) >>> complex('-4.5j') -4.5j >>> complex('-1.23+4.5j') (-1.23+4.5j) >>> complex('\t( -1.23+4.5J )\n') (-1.23+4.5j) >>> complex('-Infinity+NaNj') (-inf+nanj) >>> complex(1.23) (1.23+0j) >>> -4.5j >>> complex(-1.23, 4.5) (-1.23+4.5j) @17,10546,663,61
    text If the argument is a string, it must contain either a real part (in the same format as for ) or an imaginary part (in the same format but with a or suffix), or both real and imaginary parts (the sign of the imaginary part is mandatory in this case). The string can optionally be surrounded by whitespaces and the round parentheses and , which are ignored. The string must not contain whitespace between , , the or suffix, and the decimal number. For example, is fine, but raises . More precisely, the input must conform to the production rule in the following grammar, after parentheses and leading and trailing whitespace characters are removed: @17,10619,663,142
