    text A base-n integer string contains digits, each representing a value from 0 to n-1. The values 0–9 can be represented by any Unicode decimal digit. The values 10–35 can be represented by to (or to ). The default is 10. The allowed bases are 0 and 2–36. Base-2, -8, and -16 strings can be optionally prefixed with /, /, or /, as with integer literals in code. For base 0, the string is interpreted in a similar way to an , in that the actual base is 2, 8, 10, or 16 as determined by the prefix. Base 0 also disallows leading zeros: is not legal, while and are. @17,26951,663,122
