    text Iterate over several iterables in parallel, producing tuples with an item from each one. @17,55514,663,21
    text Example: @17,55547,663,20
    text >>> for item in zip([1, 2, 3], ['sugar', 'spice', 'everything nice']): ... print(item) ... (1, 'sugar') (2, 'spice') (3, 'everything nice') @17,55579,663,41
    text More formally: zip() returns an iterator of tuples, where the i-th tuple contains the i-th element from each of the argument iterables. @17,55632,663,40
    text Another way to think of is that it turns rows into columns, and columns into rows. This is similar to . @17,55684,663,21
    text zip() @17,55717,663,20
    link "transposing a matrix" https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transpose @17,55749,663,20
    text is lazy: The elements won’t be processed until the iterable is iterated on, e.g. by a loop or by wrapping in a . @17,55781,663,41
    text zip() @17,55834,663,20
    text for @17,55866,663,20
    link list stdtypes.html#list @17,55898,663,21
    text One thing to consider is that the iterables passed to zip() could have different lengths; sometimes by design, and sometimes because of a bug in the code that prepared these iterables. Python offers three different approaches to dealing with this issue: @17,55931,663,61
    text By default, stops when the shortest iterable is . It will ignore the remaining items in the longer iterables, cutting off the result to the length of the shortest iterable: @17,56004,663,40
    row @17,56056,663,20
      text zip() @17,56056,30,20
      link exhausted ../glossary.html#term-exhausted @59,56056,69,20
    text >>> list(zip(range(3), ['fee', 'fi', 'fo', 'fum'])) [(0, 'fee'), (1, 'fi'), (2, 'fo')] @17,56088,663,21
    text zip() is often used in cases where the iterables are assumed to be of equal length. In such cases, it’s recommended to use the option. Its output is the same as regular zip(): @17,56121,663,40
    text >>> list(zip(('a', 'b', 'c'), (1, 2, 3), [('a', 1), ('b', 2), ('c', 3)] @17,56173,663,21
    text Unlike the default behavior, it raises a if one iterable is before the others: @17,56206,663,20
    row @17,56238,663,20
      link ValueError exceptions.html#ValueError @17,56238,70,20
      link exhausted ../glossary.html#term-exhausted @99,56238,69,20
    text >>> for item in zip(range(3), ['fee', 'fi', 'fo', 'fum'], ... print(item) ... (0, 'fee') (1, 'fi') (2, 'fo') Traceback (most recent call last): ... ValueError: zip() argument 2 is longer than argument 1 @17,56270,663,41
    text Without the argument, any bug that results in iterables of different lengths will be silenced, possibly manifesting as a hard-to-find bug in another part of the program. @17,56323,663,40
    text Shorter iterables can be padded with a constant value to make all the iterables have the same length. This is done by . @17,56375,663,41
    link itertools.zip_longest() ../library/itertools.html#itertools.zip_longest @17,56428,663,20
    text Edge cases: With a single iterable argument, zip() returns an iterator of 1-tuples. With no arguments, it returns an empty iterator. @17,56460,663,41
    text Tips and tricks: @17,56513,663,20
    text The left-to-right evaluation order of the iterables is guaranteed. This makes possible an idiom for clustering a data series into n-length groups using zip(*[iter(s)]*n, This repeats the same iterator n times so that each output tuple has the result of n calls to the iterator. This has the effect of dividing the input into n-length chunks. @17,56545,663,81
    text zip() in conjunction with the * operator can be used to unzip a list: @17,56638,663,21
    text >>> x  [1, 2, 3] >>> y  [4, 5, 6] >>> list(zip(x, y)) [(1, 4), (2, 5), (3, 6)] >>> x2, y2  zip(*zip(x, y)) >>> x = list(x2) and y = list(y2) True @17,56671,663,40
    text Changed in version 3.10: Added the strict argument. @17,56723,663,21
