    link "PEP 328" https://peps.python.org/pep-0328/ @17,57873,663,20
    text When the name variable is of the form package.module, normally, the top-level package (the name up till the first dot) is returned, not the module named by name. However, when a non-empty fromlist argument is given, the module named by name is returned. @17,57905,663,61
    text For example, the statement import spam results in bytecode resembling the following code: @17,57978,663,20
    text spam  __import__('spam', globals(), locals(), [], 0) @17,58010,663,21
    text The statement import spam.ham results in this call: @17,58043,663,20
    text spam  __import__('spam.ham', globals(), locals(), [], 0) @17,58075,663,20
    text Note how returns the toplevel module here because this is the object that is bound to a name by the statement. @17,58107,663,41
    text __import__() @17,58160,663,20
    link import ../reference/simple_stmts.html#import @17,58192,663,20
    text On the other hand, the statement from spam.ham import eggs, sausage as saus results in @17,58224,663,21
    text _temp  __import__('spam.ham', globals(), locals(), ['eggs', 'sausage'], 0) eggs  _temp.eggs saus  _temp.sausage @17,58257,663,40
    text Here, the spam.ham module is returned from __import__(). From this object, the names to import are retrieved and assigned to their respective names. @17,58309,663,41
    text If you simply want to import a module (potentially within a package) by name, use . @17,58362,663,20
